@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title Book

@s From a Manifest to a Program

Parser taught us how one `.lit` file becomes one source-located chapter. A
book begins with a smaller file called a **manifest**. The manifest names the
book, supplies settings shared by its chapters, and lists those chapter files
in reading order. Suppose `greetings.lit` contains:

```text
@book
@title Greetings
@code_type text .txt
@comment_type none

This book contains three short greetings.

[English](english.lit)
    [A friendlier greeting](friendly.lit)
[Spanish](spanish.lit)
```

The link labels are the short names used in book navigation. The paths identify
files beside the manifest. Indenting an entry makes it a minor chapter under
the preceding unindented entry, so these three chapters receive the numbers 1,
1.1, and 2.

Book processing has two deliberately separate transformations. Pure manifest
parsing first records what these lines request without opening a chapter file.
Loading then reads and parses the three files and builds the same @code{Program}
shape used for one-file input.

@s The Parsed Manifest

The first transformation produces this source-level description:

```text
BookManifest "greetings.lit"
|-- title "Greetings"                               line 2
|-- commands
|   |-- code type "text .txt"                       line 3
|   `-- comment type ""                             line 4
|-- introduction "This book contains..."            line 6
`-- chapter entries
    |-- 1   "English"               english.lit     line 8
    |-- 1.1 "A friendlier greeting" friendly.lit    line 9
    `-- 2   "Spanish"               spanish.lit     line 10
```

Blank introduction lines are retained with their source locations even though
the compact tree shows only its sentence. The empty comment pattern comes from
the special value `none`. Output backends later choose their own destinations
from the manifest and chapter source paths.

Nothing has yet tried to read `english.lit`. This separation lets malformed
paths, duplicate entries, missing required fields, and other predictable
manifest problems be reported before any chapter read is attempted.

@s Loading the Chapters

After a manifest is valid, loading resolves each chapter path relative to the
manifest's directory. For example, `english.lit` might contain:

```text
@title An English Greeting
@s Greeting
--- english.txt
Hello
---
```

Parser handles that file exactly as it handled the running example in the
previous chapter. The manifest's `@code_type` and `@comment_type` settings
apply before any settings written in the chapter, so the `english.txt`
occurrence captures code type `text .txt` and an empty comment pattern.

After all three files parse successfully, loading returns:

```text
Program "Greetings"                                 kind Book
|-- BookMetadata                                    introduction lines
|-- Chapter 1   navigation "English"                source english.lit
|               title "An English Greeting"
|-- Chapter 1.1 navigation "A friendlier greeting"  source friendly.lit
|                                               
`-- Chapter 2   navigation "Spanish"                source spanish.lit
```

The manifest label and a chapter's own title have different jobs: the shorter
label appears in navigation, while the declared title heads the chapter page.
All chapters now share one ordered @code{Program}, which Resolver, Tangler,
Identifier, and Weaver can process without reopening the manifest.

@s The Book Module

Book support has a separate generated module even though several of its model
values extend the Parser types. The module separates manifest parsing from the
functions that read files.

--- src/book.rs
//! Pure book-manifest parsing and explicit chapter loading.

@{Book imports}
@{Book manifest model}
@{Book error model}
@{Render one book error}
@{Book error collection}
@{Manifest parse entry points}
@{Parse a book manifest source}
@{Append one manifest introduction line}
@{Validate and number one manifest chapter}
@{Check the manifest filename}
@{Book entry parser}
@{Recognize a book manifest}
@{Book loading entry points}
@{Load manifest chapters}
---

@s The Manifest Model

@code{BookManifest} represents the complete first tree shown above, before any
chapter is opened. It keeps the original source as well as the title, shared
commands, introduction, and ordered entries derived from it.
Each @code{ManifestChapter} is one line such as
`[English](english.lit)`, expanded with its source location and assigned
number.

These values describe requests that have been checked but not yet performed.
After loading succeeds, their information moves into the general
@code{Program} and @code{Chapter} model taught by Parser.

--- Book manifest model
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct BookManifest {
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub title: String,
    pub title_origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub commands: Vec<Command>,
    pub introduction: String,
    pub introduction_lines: Vec<Line>,
    pub chapters: Vec<ManifestChapter>,
    pub file: String,
    pub text: String,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ManifestChapter {
    pub navigation_label: String,
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub source_path: PathBuf,
    pub major_number: usize,
    pub minor_number: usize,
}
---

@s Parsing a Manifest

Manifest commands and chapter entries must occupy complete lines. Safe
configuration commands such as lines 3-4 of `greetings.lit` are retained in
source order for chapters to inherit. The title and exact `@book` marker are
required. Lines that are neither a recognized command nor a complete chapter
entry become introduction prose for the contents page.

The parser assigns major numbers to unindented entries and minor numbers to
indented entries, validates paths and duplicates before loading, and collects
source-ordered failures rather than stopping at the first bad line.

--- Manifest parse entry points
pub fn parse_book_manifest_bytes(
    file: impl Into<String>,
    source: &[u8],
) -> Result<BookManifest, BookErrors> {
    let file = file.into();
    match std::str::from_utf8(source) {
        Ok(source) => parse_book_manifest_source(file, source),
        Err(error) => {
            let valid_up_to = error.valid_up_to();
            let line = source[..valid_up_to]
                .iter()
                .filter(|byte| **byte == b'\n')
                .count()
                + 1;
            Err(BookErrors {
                errors: vec![BookError {
                    origin: SourceOrigin::new(file, line),
                    kind: BookErrorKind::InvalidUtf8 { valid_up_to },
                }],
            })
        }
    }
}

pub fn parse_book_manifest_str(
    file: impl Into<String>,
    source: &str,
) -> Result<BookManifest, BookErrors> {
    parse_book_manifest_source(file.into(), source)
}
---

@s The Manifest Source Parser

The private source entry point initializes the in-progress model, reads each
line once, and either returns the completed manifest or its ordered errors.

--- Parse a book manifest source
fn parse_book_manifest_source(
    file: String,
    source: &str,
) -> Result<BookManifest, BookErrors> {
    @{Initialize manifest parsing}
    @{Read manifest commands and introduction prose}
    @{Finish the manifest}
}
---

@s Initializing Manifest Parsing

The parser begins with one variable for every field it is collecting, plus the
current chapter numbers, the set of paths already seen, and an error list. A
usable `.lit` filename is required because every backend must be able to derive
an output name from the manifest.

--- Initialize manifest parsing
let origin = SourceOrigin::new(&file, 1);
let mut errors = Vec::new();
let mut book_marker = None;
let mut title = None;
let mut commands = Vec::new();
let mut introduction = String::new();
let mut introduction_lines = Vec::new();
let mut chapters = Vec::new();
let mut chapter_sequence = ChapterSequence::default();
let source_lines: Vec<_> = source
    .split('\n')
    .map(|line| line.strip_suffix('\r').unwrap_or(line))
    .collect();
let fenced_lines = fenced_line_mask(&source_lines);
let table_lines = table_line_mask(&source_lines);

if !valid_manifest_path(&file) {
    errors.push(BookError {
        origin: origin.clone(),
        kind: BookErrorKind::InvalidManifestPath {
            path: PathBuf::from(&file),
        },
    });
}
---

@s Reading Manifest Lines

Lines inside a valid prose fence or table-shaped range go directly to the
introduction, so an example cannot become a manifest command or chapter entry.
Every other line is tried first as a command and then as a complete
Markdown-shaped entry.

The chapter-entry match contains every outcome. Ordinary prose and
non-`.lit` links join the introduction, malformed entries become errors, and a
`.lit` candidate is delegated as one complete validation operation.

--- Read manifest commands and introduction prose
for (index, line) in source_lines.iter().copied().enumerate() {
    let line_origin = SourceOrigin::new(&file, index + 1);

    if fenced_lines[index] || table_lines[index] {
        append_introduction_line(
            &mut introduction,
            &mut introduction_lines,
            line,
            line_origin,
        );
        continue;
    }

    if let Some((name, arguments)) = command_parts(line) {
        match name {
            "@book" => {
                let duplicate = book_marker.replace(index + 1).is_some();
                if !arguments.is_empty() {
                    errors.push(BookError {
                        origin: line_origin.clone(),
                        kind: BookErrorKind::BookMarkerArguments,
                    });
                }
                if duplicate {
                    errors.push(BookError {
                        origin: line_origin,
                        kind: BookErrorKind::DuplicateBookMarker,
                    });
                }
                continue;
            }
            "@title" => {
                if title.is_some() {
                    errors.push(BookError {
                        origin: line_origin,
                        kind: BookErrorKind::DuplicateTitle,
                    });
                } else if arguments.is_empty() {
                    errors.push(BookError {
                        origin: line_origin.clone(),
                        kind: BookErrorKind::EmptyTitle,
                    });
                    title = Some((String::new(), line_origin));
                } else {
                    title = Some((arguments.to_owned(), line_origin));
                }
                continue;
            }
            "@code_type" | "@comment_type" | "@colorscheme" => {
                if arguments.is_empty() {
                    errors.push(BookError {
                        origin: line_origin,
                        kind: BookErrorKind::MissingCommandArguments {
                            name: name.to_owned(),
                        },
                    });
                } else {
                    commands.push(Command {
                        kind: match name {
                            "@code_type" => CommandKind::CodeType,
                            "@comment_type" => CommandKind::CommentType,
                            "@colorscheme" => CommandKind::ColorScheme,
                            _ => {
                                unreachable!("the match arm selected a known command")
                            }
                        },
                        name: name.to_owned(),
                        arguments: if arguments == "none" {
                            String::new()
                        } else {
                            arguments.to_owned()
                        },
                        origin: line_origin,
                    });
                }
                continue;
            }
            "@s" => {
                errors.push(BookError {
                    origin: line_origin,
                    kind: BookErrorKind::UnsupportedCommand {
                        name: name.to_owned(),
                    },
                });
                continue;
            }
            name if is_deferred_command(name) => {
                errors.push(BookError {
                    origin: line_origin,
                    kind: BookErrorKind::UnsupportedCommand {
                        name: name.to_owned(),
                    },
                });
                continue;
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }

    match chapter_entry(line) {
        EntryLine::Prose => append_introduction_line(
            &mut introduction,
            &mut introduction_lines,
            line,
            line_origin,
        ),
        EntryLine::Malformed => errors.push(BookError {
            origin: line_origin,
            kind: BookErrorKind::MalformedChapterEntry {
                text: line.to_owned(),
            },
        }),
        EntryLine::Candidate {
            indented,
            label,
            target,
        } => match validate_and_number_chapter(
            indented,
            label,
            target,
            &line_origin,
            &mut chapter_sequence,
            &mut errors,
        ) {
            CandidateResult::Introduction => append_introduction_line(
                &mut introduction,
                &mut introduction_lines,
                line,
                line_origin,
            ),
            CandidateResult::Chapter(chapter) => chapters.push(chapter),
            CandidateResult::Invalid => {}
        },
    }
}
---

@s Retaining Manifest Introduction Lines

One helper keeps the convenient introduction string and its source-located
lines in step. Blank lines and ordinary non-chapter links use the same path as
prose protected by a fence or table.

--- Append one manifest introduction line
fn append_introduction_line(
    introduction: &mut String,
    lines: &mut Vec<Line>,
    text: &str,
    origin: SourceOrigin,
) {
    introduction.push_str(text);
    introduction.push('\n');
    lines.push(Line {
        origin,
        text: text.to_owned(),
    });
}
---

@s Validating and Numbering One Chapter

A Markdown link becomes a chapter only when its target looks like a `.lit`
path. Other complete links remain introduction prose. For a chapter candidate,
the helper validates its label and portable path, detects duplicates, advances
the major or minor number, derives the HTML path, and checks that the chapter
will not replace the contents page. The path set and two counters travel
together as the state of the chapter sequence.

@code{CandidateResult} distinguishes those three outcomes without making the
line-reading loop reproduce the validation details.

--- Validate and number one manifest chapter
enum CandidateResult {
    Introduction,
    Chapter(ManifestChapter),
    Invalid,
}

#[derive(Default)]
struct ChapterSequence {
    paths: HashSet<PathBuf>,
    major_number: usize,
    minor_number: usize,
}

fn validate_and_number_chapter(
    indented: bool,
    label: String,
    target: String,
    origin: &SourceOrigin,
    sequence: &mut ChapterSequence,
    errors: &mut Vec<BookError>,
) -> CandidateResult {
    if label.is_empty() {
        errors.push(BookError {
            origin: origin.clone(),
            kind: BookErrorKind::EmptyChapterLabel,
        });
    }
    if target.is_empty() {
        errors.push(BookError {
            origin: origin.clone(),
            kind: BookErrorKind::EmptyChapterPath,
        });
        return CandidateResult::Invalid;
    }

    let target_without_separator = target.trim_end_matches(['/', '\\']);
    let looks_like_chapter = Path::new(&target).extension() == Some(OsStr::new("lit"))
        || Path::new(target_without_separator).extension() == Some(OsStr::new("lit"));
    if !looks_like_chapter {
        return CandidateResult::Introduction;
    }

    let source_path = match validate_relative_path(&target) {
        Ok(path) => path,
        Err(reason) => {
            errors.push(BookError {
                origin: origin.clone(),
                kind: BookErrorKind::InvalidChapterPath {
                    path: PathBuf::from(target),
                    reason,
                },
            });
            return CandidateResult::Invalid;
        }
    };
    if !sequence.paths.insert(source_path.clone()) {
        errors.push(BookError {
            origin: origin.clone(),
            kind: BookErrorKind::DuplicateChapterPath {
                path: source_path.clone(),
            },
        });
    }

    if indented {
        if sequence.major_number == 0 {
            errors.push(BookError {
                origin: origin.clone(),
                kind: BookErrorKind::MinorBeforeMajor,
            });
        }
        sequence.minor_number += 1;
    } else {
        sequence.major_number += 1;
        sequence.minor_number = 0;
    }

    CandidateResult::Chapter(ManifestChapter {
        navigation_label: label,
        origin: origin.clone(),
        source_path,
        major_number: sequence.major_number,
        minor_number: sequence.minor_number,
    })
}
---

@s Finishing the Manifest

After the pass, the manifest parser checks that the marker, title, and at least
one chapter were present. Only a complete error-free manifest becomes
@code{BookManifest}; otherwise errors are sorted by source line and returned
together.

--- Finish the manifest
if book_marker.is_none() {
    errors.push(BookError {
        origin: origin.clone(),
        kind: BookErrorKind::MissingBookMarker,
    });
}
if title.is_none() {
    errors.push(BookError {
        origin: origin.clone(),
        kind: BookErrorKind::MissingTitle,
    });
}
if chapters.is_empty() {
    errors.push(BookError {
        origin: origin.clone(),
        kind: BookErrorKind::NoChapters,
    });
}

if !errors.is_empty() {
    errors.sort_by_key(|error| error.origin.line);
    return Err(BookErrors { errors });
}

let (title, title_origin) = title.expect("missing title returned above");
Ok(BookManifest {
    origin,
    title,
    title_origin,
    commands,
    introduction,
    introduction_lines,
    chapters,
    file,
    text: source.to_owned(),
})
---

@s Checking the Manifest Filename

The manifest must have a nonempty `.lit` filename. This is an input invariant,
not a choice of output format: HTML and LaTeX will later derive different
destinations from the same checked name.

--- Check the manifest filename
fn valid_manifest_path(file: &str) -> bool {
    let Some(filename) = Path::new(file).file_name() else {
        return false;
    };
    let filename_path = Path::new(filename);
    filename_path.extension() == Some(OsStr::new("lit"))
        && filename_path
            .file_stem()
            .is_some_and(|stem| !stem.is_empty())
}
---

@s Recognizing Chapter Entries

A chapter entry is deliberately a small Markdown-link grammar, not arbitrary
inline Markdown. It must occupy one complete line. Leading whitespace records
minor-chapter indentation; surrounding whitespace on the label and target is
removed. A complete link to a non-`.lit` target remains introduction prose,
while an incomplete link-shaped line is malformed.

--- Book entry parser
enum EntryLine {
    Prose,
    Malformed,
    Candidate {
        indented: bool,
        label: String,
        target: String,
    },
}

fn chapter_entry(line: &str) -> EntryLine {
    let without_trailing = line.trim_end_matches([' ', '\t']);
    let content = without_trailing.trim_start_matches([' ', '\t']);
    let indented = content.len() != without_trailing.len();
    if !content.starts_with('[') {
        return EntryLine::Prose;
    }

    let Some(label_end) = content.find(']') else {
        return EntryLine::Malformed;
    };
    if content.as_bytes().get(label_end + 1) != Some(&b'(') {
        return EntryLine::Prose;
    }
    let Some(target_end) = content[label_end + 2..].find(')') else {
        return EntryLine::Malformed;
    };
    let target_end = label_end + 2 + target_end;
    if target_end + 1 != content.len() {
        return EntryLine::Prose;
    }

    EntryLine::Candidate {
        indented,
        label: content[1..label_end].trim().to_owned(),
        target: content[label_end + 2..target_end].trim().to_owned(),
    }
}
---

@s Recognizing a Book Manifest

The CLI selects book mode only when the bytes contain an active exact
standalone `@book` marker. A marker shown inside a valid prose fence or
table-shaped range is literal; an unclosed fence shields nothing. Trailing
horizontal whitespace and CRLF are accepted, while a prefix, arguments, or
text embedded in another line is not enough.

--- Recognize a book manifest
pub fn is_book_manifest(source: &[u8]) -> bool {
    let exact_marker = |line: &[u8]| {
        let line = line.strip_suffix(b"\r").unwrap_or(line);
        let end = line
            .iter()
            .rposition(|byte| !matches!(byte, b' ' | b'\t'))
            .map_or(0, |index| index + 1);
        &line[..end] == b"@book"
    };
    let Ok(text) = std::str::from_utf8(source) else {
        return source.split(|byte| *byte == b'\n').any(exact_marker);
    };
    let lines: Vec<_> = text
        .split('\n')
        .map(|line| line.strip_suffix('\r').unwrap_or(line))
        .collect();
    let fenced_lines = fenced_line_mask(&lines);
    let table_lines = table_line_mask(&lines);
    source
        .split(|byte| *byte == b'\n')
        .zip(fenced_lines.into_iter().zip(table_lines))
        .any(|(line, (fenced, table))| !fenced && !table && exact_marker(line))
}
---

@s Book Chapter Metadata

Each loaded chapter gains the navigation label and entry origin written in the
manifest, plus its validated source path. For the first example entry, those
values are `English`, line 8, and `english.lit`. Keeping them in an optional
value lets the ordinary @code{Chapter} model serve both one-file and book input.

--- Book chapter metadata
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct BookChapter {
    pub navigation_label: String,
    pub label_origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub source_path: PathBuf,
}
---

@s Book Metadata on the Program

The program-wide book value records the manifest introduction in both
convenient text and source-located line forms. @code{ProgramKind::Book} carries
this value while each chapter retains its source and navigation metadata.

--- Book metadata
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct BookMetadata {
    pub introduction: String,
    pub introduction_lines: Vec<Line>,
}
---

@s Parsing a Loaded Book Chapter

The book-only byte entry point adds inherited commands, manifest numbering,
and page metadata to the common chapter parser. That is how `english.txt`
captures the manifest's `text .txt` setting even though `english.lit` does not
repeat the command. UTF-8 validation remains at the byte boundary, exactly as
it does for a single file.

--- Parse a loaded book chapter
pub(crate) fn parse_chapter_bytes(
    file: String,
    source: &[u8],
    inherited_commands: Vec<Command>,
    major_number: usize,
    minor_number: usize,
    book: BookChapter,
) -> Result<Chapter, ParseErrors> {
    match std::str::from_utf8(source) {
        Ok(source) => parse_chapter_source(
            file,
            source,
            inherited_commands,
            major_number,
            minor_number,
            Some(book),
        ),
        Err(error) => Err(invalid_utf8_error(file, source, error.valid_up_to())),
    }
}
---

@s Book Loading Entry Points

Only a valid complete manifest reaches chapter loading. @code{load_book} reads a
manifest path itself; @code{load_book_bytes} reuses bytes already read by the CLI.
Both retain the manifest path as the base for later relative chapter reads.
In either case, pure parsing finishes before @code{load_manifest} begins those
reads.

--- Book loading entry points
pub fn load_book(path: impl AsRef<Path>) -> Result<Program, BookErrors> {
    let path = path.as_ref();
    let file = path.to_string_lossy().into_owned();
    let source = fs::read(path).map_err(|error| BookErrors {
        errors: vec![BookError {
            origin: SourceOrigin::new(&file, 1),
            kind: BookErrorKind::ReadManifest {
                path: path.to_path_buf(),
                kind: error.kind(),
            },
        }],
    })?;
    load_book_bytes(path, &source)
}

pub fn load_book_bytes(
    path: impl AsRef<Path>,
    source: &[u8],
) -> Result<Program, BookErrors> {
    let path = path.as_ref();
    let file = path.to_string_lossy().into_owned();
    let manifest = parse_book_manifest_bytes(file, source)?;
    load_manifest(path, manifest)
}
---

@s Loading Manifest Chapters

Paths are resolved relative to the manifest's directory. For each entry in
order, the loader reads its bytes, prepares the @code{BookChapter} metadata,
and calls Parser with the manifest commands and assigned number. Successful
chapters join the final @code{Program} in that same order.

The loader attempts every chapter even when one read or parse fails, so it can
report independent failures deterministically. It returns no partial
@code{Program} when any chapter failed.

--- Load manifest chapters
fn load_manifest(
    manifest_path: &Path,
    manifest: BookManifest,
) -> Result<Program, BookErrors> {
    let directory = manifest_path.parent().unwrap_or_else(|| Path::new(""));
    let mut chapters = Vec::with_capacity(manifest.chapters.len());
    let mut errors = Vec::new();

    for entry in &manifest.chapters {
        let chapter_path = directory.join(&entry.source_path);
        let source = match fs::read(&chapter_path) {
            Ok(source) => source,
            Err(error) => {
                errors.push(BookError {
                    origin: entry.origin.clone(),
                    kind: BookErrorKind::ReadChapter {
                        path: entry.source_path.clone(),
                        kind: error.kind(),
                    },
                });
                continue;
            }
        };
        let book = BookChapter {
            navigation_label: entry.navigation_label.clone(),
            label_origin: entry.origin.clone(),
            source_path: entry.source_path.clone(),
        };
        match parse_chapter_bytes(
            chapter_path.to_string_lossy().into_owned(),
            &source,
            manifest.commands.clone(),
            entry.major_number,
            entry.minor_number,
            book,
        ) {
            Ok(chapter) => chapters.push(chapter),
            Err(parse_errors) => errors.push(BookError {
                origin: entry.origin.clone(),
                kind: BookErrorKind::ChapterParse {
                    path: entry.source_path.clone(),
                    errors: parse_errors,
                },
            }),
        }
    }

    if !errors.is_empty() {
        return Err(BookErrors { errors });
    }

    Ok(Program {
        kind: ProgramKind::Book(BookMetadata {
            introduction: manifest.introduction,
            introduction_lines: manifest.introduction_lines,
        }),
        origin: manifest.origin,
        title: manifest.title,
        title_origin: Some(manifest.title_origin),
        commands: manifest.commands,
        chapters,
        file: manifest.file,
        text: manifest.text,
    })
}
---

@s Book Diagnostics

A manifest failure points to the manifest line that made the request. A
chapter read error therefore points to its entry; a chapter parse error retains
both that entry context and the chapter's own internal origins. I/O failures
store portable @code{io::ErrorKind} values. As with one-file parsing, errors are
structured and no partial program is returned.

--- Book error model
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct BookError {
    pub origin: SourceOrigin,
    pub kind: BookErrorKind,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum BookErrorKind {
    ReadManifest {
        path: PathBuf,
        kind: io::ErrorKind,
    },
    InvalidUtf8 {
        valid_up_to: usize,
    },
    MissingBookMarker,
    DuplicateBookMarker,
    BookMarkerArguments,
    MissingTitle,
    DuplicateTitle,
    EmptyTitle,
    NoChapters,
    MissingCommandArguments {
        name: String,
    },
    UnsupportedCommand {
        name: String,
    },
    MalformedChapterEntry {
        text: String,
    },
    EmptyChapterLabel,
    EmptyChapterPath,
    MinorBeforeMajor,
    InvalidManifestPath {
        path: PathBuf,
    },
    InvalidChapterPath {
        path: PathBuf,
        reason: InvalidPathReason,
    },
    DuplicateChapterPath {
        path: PathBuf,
    },
    ReadChapter {
        path: PathBuf,
        kind: io::ErrorKind,
    },
    ChapterParse {
        path: PathBuf,
        errors: ParseErrors,
    },
}
---

@s Rendering One Book Error

One book error combines its manifest-side origin with the structured failure.
A chapter parse failure retains the nested parse collection instead of
flattening away the chapter's own source locations.

--- Render one book error
impl fmt::Display for BookError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}: error: ", self.origin)?;
        match &self.kind {
            BookErrorKind::ReadManifest { path, kind } => {
                write!(f, "cannot read book manifest {}: {kind}", path.display())
            }
            BookErrorKind::InvalidUtf8 { valid_up_to } => {
                write!(f, "book manifest is not UTF-8 at byte {valid_up_to}")
            }
            BookErrorKind::MissingBookMarker => {
                f.write_str("book manifest has no @book marker")
            }
            BookErrorKind::DuplicateBookMarker => {
                f.write_str("book manifest has more than one @book marker")
            }
            BookErrorKind::BookMarkerArguments => {
                f.write_str("the @book marker does not accept arguments")
            }
            BookErrorKind::MissingTitle => f.write_str("book manifest has no @title"),
            BookErrorKind::DuplicateTitle => {
                f.write_str("book manifest has more than one @title")
            }
            BookErrorKind::EmptyTitle => f.write_str("book title is empty"),
            BookErrorKind::NoChapters => f.write_str("book manifest has no chapters"),
            BookErrorKind::MissingCommandArguments { name } => {
                write!(f, "command {name} requires arguments")
            }
            BookErrorKind::UnsupportedCommand { name } => {
                write!(f, "command {name} is not supported in a book manifest")
            }
            BookErrorKind::MalformedChapterEntry { text } => {
                write!(f, "malformed chapter entry: {text}")
            }
            BookErrorKind::EmptyChapterLabel => {
                f.write_str("chapter navigation label is empty")
            }
            BookErrorKind::EmptyChapterPath => f.write_str("chapter path is empty"),
            BookErrorKind::MinorBeforeMajor => {
                f.write_str("a minor chapter must follow a major chapter")
            }
            BookErrorKind::InvalidManifestPath { path } => {
                write!(
                    f,
                    "book manifest must have a usable .lit filename: {}",
                    path.display()
                )
            }
            BookErrorKind::InvalidChapterPath { path, reason } => {
                write!(f, "invalid chapter path {}: {reason}", path.display())
            }
            BookErrorKind::DuplicateChapterPath { path } => {
                write!(
                    f,
                    "chapter path is listed more than once: {}",
                    path.display()
                )
            }
            BookErrorKind::ReadChapter { path, kind } => {
                write!(f, "cannot read chapter {}: {kind}", path.display())
            }
            BookErrorKind::ChapterParse { path, errors } => {
                write!(f, "cannot parse chapter {}: {errors}", path.display())
            }
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for BookError {}
---

@s Collecting Book Errors

Book parsing and loading collect independent failures in deterministic source
or manifest order. The aggregate offers the complete collection and a compact
first-error display.

--- Book error collection
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct BookErrors {
    errors: Vec<BookError>,
}

impl BookErrors {
    pub fn as_slice(&self) -> &[BookError] {
        &self.errors
    }

    pub fn into_vec(self) -> Vec<BookError> {
        self.errors
    }
}

impl fmt::Display for BookErrors {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
        if let Some(first) = self.errors.first() {
            write!(f, "{first}")?;
            if self.errors.len() > 1 {
                write!(f, " (and {} more book errors)", self.errors.len() - 1)?;
            }
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for BookErrors {}
---

@s The Book Program Is Ready

Book has performed two transformations without mixing their effects. Manifest
parsing produced a checked list of requests without reading chapters. Loading
then read and parsed every requested file before returning one
source-located @code{Program}.

The result preserves manifest order, shared commands, major and minor numbers,
navigation labels, page paths, and both manifest-side and chapter-side
origins. Resolver can now treat all chapters as one program while still using
those origins and chapter boundaries for local name lookup. Tangler and Weaver
will likewise plan complete book-wide results before Output writes anything.

@s Book Module Dependencies

The book module uses sets and path inspection to validate manifest entries,
filesystem and I/O types at the loading boundary, Parser's shared models and
chapter entry point, and Resolver's portable relative-path contract.

--- Book imports
use std::collections::HashSet;
use std::ffi::OsStr;
use std::fmt;
use std::fs;
use std::io;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

use crate::inline::{fenced_line_mask, table_line_mask};
use crate::parser::{
    BookChapter, BookMetadata, Command, CommandKind, Line, ParseErrors, Program,
    ProgramKind, SourceOrigin, command_parts, is_deferred_command, parse_chapter_bytes,
};
use crate::resolver::{InvalidPathReason, validate_relative_path};
---
